<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toruń</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thorn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toruń is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1233 under the German name Thorn, it became a prosperous Hanseatic town. Its citizens joined the revolt against the Order in 1454, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> of 1466 placed it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>; it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1793 and returned to Poland in 1920. The birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, its medieval old town is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torun.jpg" alt="Toruń" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domun · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stare_Miasto,_Ratusz_Staromiejski_w_Toruniu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
